[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Revocation lists (CRL) (/docs/pr-preview/pr-44/platform/pki/crl)\n\n\n\n<span id=\"crl-service\" />\n\nA CRL is an issuer-signed list of revoked certificates. This guide is for an operator publishing lists and an integrator using them locally. You need the issuing CA certificate, its SKI and access to the published endpoint. The client must maintain a current copy: offline operation still requires refreshing evidence.\n\nVA role and per-CA configuration [#va-role-and-per-ca-configuration]\n\nThe VA role stores configuration and metadata for the latest list published for a CA’s SKI. Generation requires a usable issuer key; do not assume an imported CA without a key has an available CRL.\n\n| `crl_options` field                | Purpose                                                                                                                                                                                | Backend initial value                          |\n| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `validity`                         | Time from `thisUpdate` to `nextUpdate`.                                                                                                                                                | 7 days.                                        |\n| `refresh_interval`                 | Stored and returned by the API, but the VA does not use it: scheduled renewal does not depend on it (see [Periodic and automatic regeneration](#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration)). | 6 days and 23 hours.                           |\n| `regenerate_on_revoke`             | Request regeneration on revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` events.                                                                                                      | `true`.                                        |\n| `signer_certificate_serial_number` | Serial number of the existing certificate that signs the CRL.                                                                                                                          | Serial number of the CA’s current certificate. |\n\nThe signer must be an existing certificate with the `cRLSign` key usage and the same subject and public key as the CA. Certificates from before a reissue qualify. A delegated signer is not supported: a certificate with the CA’s subject but a different key is rejected.\n\nWhen a CA is reissued, the role keeps pointing at the previous certificate. CRLs remain valid because both certificates share subject and key, but the previous certificate cannot be deleted while the role uses it. To retire it, update the role’s signer.\n\n<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Cases in which the role can no longer be edited\">\n  When a CA is deleted, its VA role is kept and cannot be removed through the API. If no other CA with the same SKI remains, the role can be read but any `PUT` returns 404, and the renewal job logs a warning on every cycle because it no longer finds the CA. The API offers no way out; it can only be fixed by editing the database. If another CA with that SKI remains, the role is still editable (see [CAs that share a key](#cas-that-share-a-key)). Adjust validity and signer before deleting the CA.\n\n  A CA whose certificate lacks `cRLSign` cannot generate its CRL or edit its role (400 error). Since 4.0 the VA requires this usage. Check before importing with `openssl x509 -noout -ext keyUsage` and, if it is missing, obtain a certificate for the same CA and key that includes it and set it as the signer with `PUT`. After reissuing a CA, also check the key usage of the new certificate: it may not keep the original’s usages.\n\n  `PUT` always validates the signer, even when the request does not change it. If the stored signer is no longer valid, no field of the role can be modified, not even the validity, until a valid one is set.\n</Callout>\n\n`latest_crl` contains version, validity start and end. Versions are kept as PEM in object storage at `pki/va/crl/{subject_key_id}/{version}.crl`; the public endpoint returns DER.\n\nCAs that share a key [#cas-that-share-a-key]\n\nThe VA identifies each CRL by the CA’s SKI, and the SKI is derived from its key. Two CAs with the same key have the same SKI and share a single VA role and a single CRL. This happens when a CA is created on an existing KMS key, or when a CA is imported whose certificate declares an SKI already in use. Lamassu takes the SKI of an imported certificate as-is and does not check that it corresponds to its public key. The first CA creates the role and the others keep it.\n\nThe certificates of all of them point to `/crl/{ski}`, but the published list has the first CA as issuer and only includes its revocations. A verifier compares the CRL issuer with the issuer of the certificate it validates (RFC 5280, §6.3.2) and rejects the list if they differ. Depending on its policy, the certificate is blocked (*hard-fail*) or accepted even though it is revoked (*soft-fail*). Revocations of the secondary CAs do not appear in any list.\n\nIf the first CA is deleted, CRL generation for the remaining one fails because the configured signer no longer matches a CA with that name. Until the role’s signer is updated to a certificate of the remaining CA, the last published list keeps being served until it expires (7 days by default).\n\nTo detect the problem, compare the SKI of the CAs in the inventory: two with the same SKI are affected. To fix it, create a CA with its own key and reissue its certificates; those already issued keep the old URL and are not corrected until reissued. A CA imported with an SKI already in use does not replace the existing one as CRL issuer, because the issuer is resolved from the role’s signer. However, its own certificates point to that same URL and its revocations are not published. Until Lamassu rejects these imports, restrict the permission to import CAs as an interim control; it covers only the import path. Creating a CA with **Create New CA (Existing Key)** on a KMS key another CA already uses produces the same collision, so also avoid reusing keys across CAs. A per-CA CRL and the rejection of imports whose SKI collides with an existing CA are both proposed in [lamassuiot#834](https://github.com/lamassuiot/lamassuiot/issues/834), and neither is implemented yet.\n\nPeriodic and automatic regeneration [#periodic-and-automatic-regeneration]\n\nThe VA scheduled job checks every role on each run and requests another list when the remaining validity of `latest_crl` is less than the interval between runs. The frequency is set in Helm with `services.va.job.crl.frequency` (default `0 * * * *`) and accepts a duration (`1h`) or a cron expression. A cron expression is converted into a constant interval equal to the time left until its next firing when the service starts, so it is not aligned with the clock. `refresh_interval` plays no part. Keep `validity` well above the interval: with a shorter validity, the list is regenerated on every cycle.\n\nIf generation fails (for example, because of a role without a CA or an invalid signer), the job logs the warning `something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ski>` and retries on the next cycle. There is no other renewal path until the cause is fixed, and the last published list keeps being served until it expires. All of this depends on working jobs, storage and signing.\n\nWith `regenerate_on_revoke`, the event handler requests a new CRL upon revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` to `ACTIVE`. This happens after the status change and has its own failure paths: if it fails (missing role, invalid signer), the event is retried and, once retries are exhausted, moves to the failed-events queue. Confirming revocation does not establish that the new CRL is already published or that all clients have downloaded it.\n\nCompare CRL numbers and timestamps before and after a change. Monitor expired lists and generation failures; clients must apply policy when current evidence is unavailable.\n\nCRL contents [#crl-contents]\n\nEach entry contains serial number, revocation time and reason. The generator queries certificates in `REVOKED` state for the selected CA. A reactivated certificate disappears when the list is recalculated; an earlier downloaded copy retains its contents.\n\nThe list contains a critical *Issuing Distribution Point* (OID `2.5.29.28`) with configured distribution URLs. Check that your client interprets this extension and that it matches the list’s scope. If rejected, investigate format and scope; ignoring a critical extension is not successful validation.\n\nPublic download endpoint [#public-download-endpoint]\n\nThe VA service exposes `GET /crl/{subject_key_id}` without management authorization. Use the **issuing CA’s** SKI, not the end-entity certificate’s SKI or serial. First inspect the certificate’s CDP:\n\n```bash\nopenssl x509 -in device.crt -noout -text\n```\n\nReplace the following URL with the CDP URL and review exposure in [Deployment](/docs/deployment/self-hosted/networking):\n\n```bash\nCRL_URL='http://pki.example.com/crl/REPLACE_WITH_ISSUER_SKI'\n\ncurl --fail --show-error --silent --max-time 15 \\\n  \"$CRL_URL\" -o ca.crl.der\n\nopenssl crl -inform DER -in ca.crl.der \\\n  -outform PEM -out ca.crl.pem\n\nopenssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout \\\n  -issuer -lastupdate -nextupdate -crlnumber\n\nopenssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout -text\nopenssl crl -in ca.crl.pem -noout -verify -CAfile issuer.pem\n```\n\nYou need OpenSSL 3.x and `issuer.pem` containing the direct issuer’s certificate. Require a valid signature, current timestamps and the expected issuer. For a root that issues directly:\n\n```bash\nopenssl verify -CAfile roots.pem -CRLfile ca.crl.pem \\\n  -crl_check -purpose sslclient device.crt\n```\n\n`roots.pem` contains deliberately trusted roots. For intermediates, add `-untrusted intermediates.pem` before the `device.crt` argument. `-crl_check` checks the end-entity certificate’s revocation against the local list; it does not download other lists or establish the status of the whole chain. A complete chain check needs the corresponding CRLs and a policy requiring them.\n\nThe test should accept an allowed certificate absent from the list and reject it with `certificate revoked` after publishing and using the list containing its serial.\n\nVA role management API [#va-role-management-api]\n\nThe following paths are relative to the VA service; confirm the public prefix in the Gateway. They are not the URLs of the public OCSP and CRL endpoints.\n\n| Method | Relative path        | Operation                            |\n| ------ | -------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| `GET`  | `/v1/roles/{ca-ski}` | Read `crl_options` and `latest_crl`. |\n| `PUT`  | `/v1/roles/{ca-ski}` | Replace CRL configuration.           |\n\nYou need authentication and role read or update permissions. Consult your installation’s [API reference](/docs/api-reference) and [Access control](/docs/platform/administration/access-control). Read current configuration before updating and send the full object expected by the contract: the backend replaces `crl_options`; it does not merge omitted fields.\n\nAfter a change, check saved configuration and the next list actually published. Updating the role alone does not establish regeneration or client acceptance.\n\nCommon VA role errors [#common-va-role-errors]\n\n| Symptom                                                     | Cause                                                                                                                                             | Action                                                                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `PUT /v1/roles/{ca-ski}` returns 404 and `GET` returns 200. | The role’s CA was deleted and no other CA with the same SKI remains.                                                                              | There is no API way out; it requires database intervention. Avoid it by adjusting the role before deleting the CA. |\n| `PUT` returns 400.                                          | The signer lacks `cRLSign`, or its subject and key match no CA with that SKI (for example, after deleting the first of two CAs that share a key). | Use `PUT` to set the serial number of a valid certificate of the remaining CA.                                     |\n| `PUT` returns 400 from a 3.x client.                        | `crl_options.subject_key_id_signer` was replaced by `signer_certificate_serial_number`, which is required.                                        | Update the client to send the new field.                                                                           |\n| The CRL does not change after revoking a certificate.       | `regenerate_on_revoke` is `false`, or the event failed because of a missing role or an invalid signer.                                            | Check the VA logs and the failed-events queue; fix the signer.                                                     |\n| A verifier rejects the CRL because of the issuer.           | Several CAs share an SKI.                                                                                                                         | See [CAs that share a key](#cas-that-share-a-key).                                                                 |\n\nFrom the interface [#from-the-interface]\n\nThe CA view lets you inspect and download the available list. Compare version, issuer and timestamps with the copy downloaded from the public endpoint. Diagnose differences using [PKI troubleshooting](/docs/platform/pki/troubleshooting).\n\nReferences [#references]\n\n* [RFC 5280: X.509 and CRL profile](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280).\n* [OpenSSL: openssl-crl](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-crl/).\n* [OpenSSL: openssl-verify](https://docs.openssl.org/3.6/man1/openssl-verify/).\n\n<Cards>\n  <Card title=\"OCSP service\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/ocsp\" />\n\n  <Card title=\"Certificate validation\" href=\"/docs/platform/pki/certificate-validation\" />\n</Cards>\n"]
